1. Basic Structure
C#:using System;
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");
    }
}
Java:public class Program {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, World!");
    }
}

Key Differences:
C# uses using for namespaces, while Java uses import.
Main in C# is capitalized and resides in a class, while Java's main is lowercase.


2. Data Types
C#:int number = 10;
string text = "Hello";
bool isTrue = true;

Java:int number = 10;
String text = "Hello";
boolean isTrue = true;

Key Differences:
C# uses string (lowercase s), while Java uses String (uppercase S).
Boolean type in C# is bool, while in Java it is boolean.

3. Console Output
C#:Console.WriteLine("Hello, " + name + "!");
Java:System.out.println("Hello, " + name + "!");

Key Differences:
C# uses Console.WriteLine, while Java uses System.out.println.

4. Properties vs Getters/Setters
C#:public class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
}
Java:public class Person {
    private String name;
    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}

Key Differences:
C# has built-in properties (get; set;), making it more concise.
Java requires explicit getter and setter methods.

5. Exception Handling
C#:try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
} catch (DivideByZeroException ex) {
    Console.WriteLine("Cannot divide by zero.");
}
Java:try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
} catch (ArithmeticException ex) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero.");
}

Key Differences:
C# has specific exceptions like DivideByZeroException.
Java uses more general exceptions like ArithmeticException.

6. Memory Management
Both languages use garbage collection, but:
C# has Dispose and using for deterministic cleanup.
Java uses try-with-resources for similar functionality.

7. Platform Dependency
Java is platform-independent via JVM.
C# is primarily Windows-focused but has cross-platform support via .NET Core.
Both languages are powerful and versatile, but C# often feels more modern and concise due to features like properties, LINQ, and async/await. 
Java, on the other hand, is widely used for its portability and extensive ecosystem.
